  "history": "Aydin Senkut left Google in 2005 \u2014 he had been its first product manager for international sites and then its first international sales manager \u2014 and began writing angel cheques the same year, founding Felicis Ventures in 2006. For four years the capital was his own: the firm's first institutional fund did not close until 2010, which is why no Felicis Ventures I files with the SEC and the vehicle ladder starts at Felicis Ventures II. That angel era is on the firm's own site under its own name, and it is where the reputation was built \u2014 Mint (Intuit, 2009), Meraki (Cisco, 2012, $1.2B), Powerset (Microsoft, 2008), Mixer Labs (Twitter, 2009), Justin.tv, which became Twitch (Amazon, 2014), Dogster, Freewebs, Plusmo and Sendori. In December 2010 Felicis put money into Shopify's $7M Series A alongside Bessemer and FirstMark, the position that still dominates the book's market value. The firm scaled deliberately: $40M for Fund II in 2010, $70M for III, $120M for IV, $200M for V, $270M for VI, $505M for VII, $600M for VIII plus a $300M Focus Fund, $825M for IX in 2023 and $900M for X in 2025. The middle book is Adyen (Series A, June 2014), Canva (Felicis led the $15M Series A in October 2015 at a $165M valuation \u2014 the largest cheque it had written to that point), Plaid, Credit Karma, Fitbit, Ring, Cruise, Bonobos, Rovio, Guardant Health, Opendoor, Warby Parker, Flexport and Weights & Biases. The current one is AI-weighted and led from the front: Felicis led Runway's $50M Series C at $500M in December 2022 (now $5.3B), led Supabase's $80M Series B in 2022 and stayed in through a $10.5B Series F, led Mercor's $100M Series B at $2B and then its $350M Series C at $10B, led n8n's Series A, and was in Periodic Labs' $300M seed at $1.3B, Poolside, Crusoe and Skild AI.",
  "investment_concept": "A generalist first-cheque firm that concentrates rather than sprays: Felicis writes seed and Series A cheques across AI, healthcare and bio, fintech, security, consumer and infrastructure, and then leads the follow-on rounds of the companies that work \u2014 Mercor's B and C, Supabase's B through F, Runway's C. The book is unusually broad by stage for a seed firm (55% seed entries, 32% Series A, 13% at Series B or later) and unusually international for a Menlo Park fund of its size, with positions in Amsterdam, Sydney, Ottawa, Dublin, Berlin, Tel Aviv, Paris, S\u00e3o Paulo, Mexico City, Madrid and Singapore.",
